      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x({x}^{2}-2x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}-2x+3)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+3=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3) \\\\  & =4-12 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$",
